x cells** — prefill and decode reported separately at three prompt lengths, every arm range overlapping. Identity: **byte-identical output, one distinct sha256 per prompt across 6 serve launches (18 completions)**. Correctness `test-backend-ops -o MUL_MAT` **1394/1394, 0 FAIL, both arms**.<br>**Kept on the distributable-engine ground, not on a measured gain:** strictly less work — one fewer workgroup barrier per row per superblock and no shared tile — for a bit-identical result.<br>**What it did NOT fix, stated:** `q6_K` mat-vec runs **~35% below `q5_K`** at the identical shape (`m=17408 n=3 k=5120`: **41.0 vs 63.8 GB/s**, ratio 0.643), every `q6_K` row in a 39.2–41.6 band. The per-row barrier was the leading mechanism for that deficit and is now **FALSIFIED**; the deficit is **OPEN**. Correction to the motivating claim: `q4_K` at that same shape is **60.6 GB/s, not 72.5** — the deficit survives on the `q5_K` comparison only. See [`FINDINGS.md`](FINDINGS.md) F-14. |
 | ROCmFP4-FAST `q8_1` MMVQ width gate | **Code, not an environment variable. No new environment variable was added for this mechanism** — the pre-existing `GGML_VK_DISABLE_MMVQ=1` is the only runtime lever, and it is upstream's, applying to every MMVQ type rather than this one (its own measured effect on `Q2_0_G128` is in the `GGML_ARIFI_G128_MMV_ROWS` row above). `ggml_vk_should_use_mmvq` returns true for `GGML_TYPE_Q4_0_ROCMFP4_FAST` on AMD at **`n == 3` and `n == 5` only**; every other width takes the f32 dequant shader. | Scope: the `q8_1` integer-dot mat-vec and mat-vec-id pipelines for `Q4_0_ROCMFP4_FAST`. Before the port the selector **listed** the type and `should_use_mmvq` **returned true**, but no line ever created the pipeline — so activations were quantized on every mat-vec, the lookup returned null, and the backend fell back to f32 in silence, paying the quantize cost for nothing. The port creates the pipelines and adds the repack and dot product (ue4m3 scale, exact-int LUT so `d_a * d_b * int-dot` forms the same product the f32 path does). | **Do not enable it more widely, and do not put this on a default branch yet.** Width 3 is the verify width of a depth-2 draft, which is where the gain is. Width 1 is a measured tie; width 4 is a tie; width 2 is the width that changed a sampled token; above 5 the port loses. | **MEASURED WIN, INTEGRATION BLOCKED.** Ladder, RIG-A, `m=17408 k=5120`, 3-round medians, epoch E2: `n=1` ×0.991, `n=2` ×1.192, **`n=3` ×1.361**, `n=4` ×1.018, **`n=5` ×1.310**, `n=8` ×1.025. Epoch E1 read ×0.998 / ×1.183 / ×1.335 / ×1.015 / ×1.316 / ×1.040 — **the gains reproduce across a driver change**. The discriminator is the control arm: under `GGML_VK_DISABLE_MMVQ=1` both arms take the identical f32 shader and every row flattens to **×0.991–1.007**, so this is the port and not build drift. Ungated at `n=8` the port read **×0.796**, which is why the gate has an upper bound at all.<br>**Serve, epoch E2** (julianmb Qwen3.8-27B-ROCmFP4-FAST